# rmmc_stranger (2026-11-27, D146; sprint plan 16.game/HEAVY-SPRINT-PLAN-SHIPPABLE-TUTORIAL-AND-VEHICLES-2026-11-27.md track A): play lesson 1 as a STRANGER who may only use what the screen says.
# The screen is the LEFT BAR of the editor, drawn from the driver's game feed (@.apps/rpg-edit-hq/board/pchq-board.xhtpm, row side-l): nick_line (who you control), stat_line (the message line: a key result for 8 s, otherwise
# the tutorial sentence), stat_line2 (the step, one line: "4/7 B: mine", "2/7 arrows 3 of 5"). NOT the gmt_* rows: the editor draws those only in the centred popup, which this book turns off (gm_pop=0); the first live run (2026-11-27) showed
# the sentence was never on screen, which is why the sentence moved to stat_line. Each step: (1) the bar must SAY the step, (2) the case presses the key the sentence NAMES by its own physical code (B = 98, the
# letter's code, never looked up in keybinds.pdl: a stranger does not read config) through the REAL driver (rmmc_driver --once, relay lines `<code> <ms>`), (3) the bar must show the next step.
# A run with no key is a LOOK: each --once run is a fresh driver, so it draws the rested screen (what the player sees 8 s after a key, when the key result has faded back to the sentence).
# rmmc_tutorial proves the grader on hand-written ledgers; rmmc_help ties the Help rows to keybinds.pdl; THIS case ties the tutorial's words to what the keys really do. Default game settings: creative OFF (D142: no play/creative.txt).
# Not covered here (the live run checks them with a photo): that the engine turns the ] key into the possession (the case sets possessed_id the way the engine's possess_next does) and that the editor draws the three lines.
RESULTS | ../results/rmmc_stranger.txt
CASE | a-stranger-finishes-lesson-1-from-the-screen-words-only
SCRATCH
INCLUDE | ../fixtures/rmmc_house.pdl
RUN | rm | -f | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/play/creative.txt
RUN | gcc | -std=gnu11 | -Wall | -Wextra | -O2 | -o | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_tutorial_op.+x | $HOUSE/@.apps/rmmc/ops/rmmc_tutorial_op.c
EXPECT_EXIT | 0 | the tutorial op compiles
RUN | gcc | -std=gnu11 | -Wall | -Wextra | -O2 | -o | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_pick_op.+x | $HOUSE/@.apps/rmmc/ops/rmmc_pick_op.c
EXPECT_EXIT | 0 | the hotbar pick op compiles
COPY | @.apps/rmmc/tutorial.pdl | h/@.apps/rmmc/tutorial.pdl
COPY | @.apps/rmmc/hotbar.pdl | h/@.apps/rmmc/hotbar.pdl
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_tutorial_op.+x | check | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc
EXPECT_EXIT | 0 | every sentence of the real lesson fits the left bar (RULE goal_max, short_max of tutorial.pdl)
EXPECT_OUT | ok: 7 steps fit | all seven steps (each goal in two bar lines, each step line in one)
MKDIR | long
WRITE | long/tutorial.pdl | LESSON \p l \p long\nSTEP \p a \p B: mine \p Press B to swing the pickaxe. The second swing breaks the block. \p mine \p 1\n
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_tutorial_op.+x | check | $T/long
EXPECT_EXIT | 1 | CONTROL: the old 64-character mine sentence (cut off on the live bar 2026-11-27) is refused
EXPECT_OUT | too long: step a goal takes 4 lines of 20 chars (max 2) | and the refusal says which row and how many lines it needs
WRITE | long/tutorial.pdl | LESSON \p l \p long\nSTEP \p a \p press ] \p Press ] (right bracket) to drive Asa. \p seat_human \p 1\nSTEP \p b \p arrows to walk far \p Walk. \p moves \p 5\n
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_tutorial_op.+x | check | $T/long
EXPECT_EXIT | 1 | CONTROL: a 37-character sentence that wraps into THREE bar lines is refused (characters alone would pass it)
EXPECT_OUT | too long: step a goal takes 3 lines | the wrap is counted the way the bar wraps words
EXPECT_OUT | too long: step b step line 29 > 20 chars: 2/2 arrows to walk far 5 of 5 | CONTROL: a step line that would wrap is refused, measured as the driver draws it
MKDIR | h/@.apps/rmmc/play/world
MKDIR | pkg/pieces/world_01
MKDIR | pkg/pieces/xelector_01
WRITE | pkg/pieces/world_01/state.txt | desk_id=rmmc-0001\n
WRITE | pkg/pieces/xelector_01/state.txt | possessed_id=\npos_z=17\n
ENV | MUCHI_TARGET_ENT | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/play/world
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_beat_op.+x | $T/h | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/beats.pdl | 1
EXPECT_OUT | spawn 140 100 | the game starts the way the book does: beat 1 puts Asa on the grass plain (the harness holds her seat)
WRITE | relay.txt |
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=waiting for keys | CONTROL: with no lesson running a fresh driver shows its idle line (the sentences below come from the lesson, not a fixed text)
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_tutorial_op.+x | start | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc | $T/led.txt | lesson1
EXPECT_OUT | started lesson1 | the Tools row "Lesson 1: drive Asa" runs this
# --- step 1: take control. The bar says "]"; that key is the engine's possess_next (keybinds KEY row 93 = ']'), which sets possessed_id; the driver turns that into a SEAT row.
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_EXIT | 0 | LOOK: the driver draws the screen with no key yet
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=Press the ] key to drive Asa. | the bar says the first step in a sentence
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=1/7 press ] | and names the key ], with the step number
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | nick_line=asa (not controlled) | and that nobody controls Asa yet
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | gm_pid= | the feed names the driver pid, so the editor watchdog can restart a dead driver (it never could for rmmc before 2026-11-27)
# (--once returns at a refused key before it rewrites the feed; the loop the editor runs goes on and shows the refusal. So the refusal is checked by its exit code and the ledger.)
WRITE | relay.txt | 98 0\n
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_EXIT | 24 | CONTROL: before taking control a game key is refused (exit 24: the seat is the harness)
EXPECT_LACKS | led.txt | DAMAGE \p asa | CONTROL: the refused key wrote nothing
EXPECT_HAS | $HOUSE/@.apps/rmmc/rmmc-pchq/pieces/system/keybinds.pdl | possess_next            \p 93 | the key the bar names, ] (code 93), is the take-control key of this book
WRITE | pkg/pieces/xelector_01/state.txt | possessed_id=rig:asa\npos_z=17\n
WRITE | relay.txt |
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_EXIT | 0 | the driver follows the possession
EXPECT_HAS | led.txt | SEAT \p asa \p human | a SEAT human row: the player controls Asa (a ledger row, not a flag)
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | nick_line=YOU CONTROL ASA | the bar says so
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=Walk five steps with the arrow keys. | and says step 2
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=2/7 arrows 0 of 5 | with the count
# --- step 2: walk. "arrow keys": the right arrow (relay code 1001). One driver run per press: the driver drops a press inside step_ms of the last step (a held key must not queue), as a person's separate taps.
WRITE | relay.txt | 1001 0\n
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_EXIT | 0 | step 1 of 5
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=2/7 arrows 1 of 5 | the bar counts 1/5
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=key right: | and the message line shows what the key did (the key result wins over the sentence while it is fresh)
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=2/7 arrows 4 of 5 | four taps, four steps
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=3/7 space: jump | five steps: the bar is on step 3
WRITE | relay.txt |
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=Press space to jump up one block. | LOOK: once the key result fades the sentence of step 3 shows
# --- step 3: jump. "space bar" = code 32.
WRITE | relay.txt | 32 0\n
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_EXIT | 0 | the jump key is accepted
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=4/7 B: dig dirt | one space press: the bar is on step 4
WRITE | relay.txt |
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=Aim at grass. Press B twice to dig dirt. | LOOK: the sentence of step 4
# --- step 4: dig dirt. "Press B" = the letter b, code 98. The sentence promises: the SECOND swing breaks it, and grass gives DIRT (the dirt steps 5 and 6 need; creative is off).
WRITE | relay.txt | 98 0\n
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_EXIT | 0 | the first swing is accepted
EXPECT_HAS | led.txt | DAMAGE \p asa | the first swing hits the block (a DAMAGE row)
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=key mine: hit, swing again | and the bar tells the player the hit landed and what to do next
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=4/7 B: dig dirt | CONTROL: one swing is not enough, the step waits, as the sentence says
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | led.txt | MINE \p asa | the second swing breaks it (a MINE row)
EXPECT_HAS | led.txt | DROP \p asa \p dirt \p 1 | and the grass dropped dirt into the bag
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=key mine: broke it: +1 dirt | the bar says it broke and what went into the bag (grass drops dirt)
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=5/7 1: pick dirt | the bar is on step 5
# --- step 5: pick. "number one" = the 1 key, code 49. A wrong number first (2) must not pass it. A pick key changes no message, so the sentence stays on the bar.
WRITE | relay.txt | 50 0\n
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=Press 1 to pick dirt in the hotbar. | the sentence of step 5
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=5/7 1: pick dirt | CONTROL: the 2 key picks another slot, the step waits
WRITE | relay.txt | 49 0\n
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_FILE | h/@.apps/rmmc/play/pick.txt | dirt\n | the 1 key picked dirt
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | gmbar_title=Blocks  (picked: dirt) | the hotbar title says dirt is picked
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=Press G to place dirt where you aim. | the bar says step 6
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=6/7 G: place | with its key
# --- step 6: place. "Press G" = code 103. Creative is OFF: the dirt must have come from the mined block (the bag), and the place costs it.
WRITE | relay.txt | 103 0\n
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_EXIT | 0 | the place is accepted with creative off
EXPECT_HAS | led.txt | PLACE \p asa \p dirt | a PLACE row for dirt
EXPECT_LACKS | led.txt | hotbar | CONTROL: no free creative GIVE row: the block came from the bag
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=key place: placed dirt | the bar says what was placed
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=7/7 Home x2: back | the bar is on step 7
WRITE | relay.txt |
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=Stuck? Press Home twice to go back. | LOOK: the sentence of step 7
# --- step 7: reset. "Home twice" = X key Home (65360) two presses; ONE press only asks (a stray tap never teleports).
WRITE | relay.txt | 65360 0\n
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=key reset: reset? press it again | CONTROL: one press asks first
EXPECT_LACKS | led.txt | RESET \p asa | and writes nothing
WRITE | relay.txt | 65360 0\n65360 0\n
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | led.txt | RESET \p asa | two presses: a RESET row
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line2=lesson done! | the bar says the lesson is done
WRITE | relay.txt |
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=Lesson complete. Well done. | LOOK: and says so in a sentence
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/vox/ops/+x/vox_op.+x | verify | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/vox | $T/led.txt
EXPECT_OUT | verified | the ledger the stranger wrote replays byte for byte
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_tutorial_op.+x | stop | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc
WRITE | relay.txt |
RUN | $T/h/@.apps/rmmc/ops/+x/rmmc_driver.+x | $T/h | $T/relay.txt | --from-start | --once | --feed | $T/feed.txt | --editor-pkg | $T/pkg
EXPECT_HAS | feed.txt | stat_line=waiting for keys | Stop: the message line is the idle line again
EXPECT_LACKS | feed.txt | stat_line2=7/7 | CONTROL: and no step is left on the bar
EXPECT_LACKS | feed.txt | Lesson complete | CONTROL: nor the lesson sentence
CLEAN |
